# ══ ONE CONVENTION IN THE REPO, WHATEVER THE MACHINE ════════════════════════
#
# Without this file, every clone's own `core.autocrlf` decides what lands in a
# commit. That is survivable while one person works here and stops being so the
# moment two do: a colleague with `autocrlf=false` commits CRLF into an index
# that holds LF, and the next diff of that file is the whole file.
#
# `text=auto` means git decides what is text and stores text as LF. The index
# already holds LF for every tracked file, so this states what is true rather
# than changing it — adding it causes no renormalisation and no churn.
#
# It deliberately does NOT set `eol`, so the working tree still follows each
# machine's `core.autocrlf`: CRLF here on Windows, LF on a Mac. What gets
# COMMITTED is the same either way, which is the part that has to be settled.
* text=auto

# ── Except where the bytes are executable ──
# A shebang with a carriage return in it is not a shebang: `/usr/bin/env
# python3\r` is a program nobody has. These stay LF in the working tree too, so
# the file works on the machine that runs it rather than only on the one that
# committed it.
*.py text eol=lf
